{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-evidence-ledger", "file": "references/failed-session-lessons.md", "content": "# Session lesson: a lucky cached guess is not a reusable rule

In a multi-demo Continual-ARC instance, the initial cached transform was an unsupported motif-copy heuristic and was rejected. The workflow then drifted into repeated demo requests without integrating full fixtures, and later candidate programs were changed repeatedly without exact regression against every pair. Similar-looking motif families varied materially: horizontal and vertical line seeds, multicolor compact patterns, differing marker colors, and unrelated sparse geometry. Some local examples were made to fit, but no unified rule was demonstrated across all six examples.

Reusable lesson: after every returned pair, stop and update a complete evidence ledger before another action. Treat selector, encoded motif, operation, placement, palette, and orientation independently. Exact-check accumulated pairs; if they do not support a common transform, another request should have a named discriminator. Never leave an identity transform or speculative heuristic in the task cache, and never describe output as validated without full-grid equality.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/HPL-hermes-luna6-low-seq0/frozen/held-out/play/users/u051/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/continual-arc-evidence-ledger/references/failed-session-lessons.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "continual-arc-solving", "file": "references/failed-guess-session-lessons.md", "content": "# Avoiding repeated speculative submissions

In a task with a cached program, an incorrect first verdict is evidence that its rule is wrong, not a reason to tweak its endpoint criteria from test appearance. Additional submissions in this session repeatedly failed because the edited transform was not checked against all demos and the inferred row selectors were unsupported. A second demo varied both grid dimensions and palette; it showed that changes are tied to specific row runs, not simply all colored rows or all trailing background. For future tasks, record exact input-output differences, derive a selector that explains every pair, run exact equality assertions on every demo, and only then submit. If these validations cannot be performed because demo grids are not represented in a runnable fixture, transcribe them and verify the relevant output rows explicitly before submitting.
